Reference energies, per-atom energies, forces and virials of MACE models, produced by
LAMMPS with the ML-MACE package (pair style mace) for the structures generated by
test/reference/structures.jl. One file per exported model:

  mpa0_f64.json    MACE-MPA-0 medium, exported in float64
  mpa0_f32.json    MACE-MPA-0 medium, exported in float32
  mp0b3_f64.json   MACE-MP-0b3 medium, exported in float64

Schema "puremlip-lammps-mace-v1"; the source checkpoints and their SHA-256 digests are
recorded in each file's "model" entry. The exported TorchScript model files are not
included. Every float is written with 17 significant digits. The pressure entry is
LAMMPS' `compute pressure NULL virial` in bar (metal units, nktv2p = 1.6021765e6).

Software:
  LAMMPS     10 Sep 2025, github.com/ACEsuit/lammps branch mace,
             commit 4d222cb3ee2a6b14083c778968497bf9e0efc4b4 (MPI, no OpenMP)
  libtorch   2.8.0 (CPU, macOS arm64) for LAMMPS
  export     torch 2.8.0, e3nn 0.4.4, numpy 2.0.2,
             mace-torch 0.3.16 at commit 4d2da09413ac1407f37cdbb6b81fa28e4c15655e,
             via mace/cli/create_lammps_model.py (--format libtorch); the float32
             export sets torch's default dtype to float32 first

Regenerate: build LAMMPS with the ML-MACE package against libtorch, export the three
models, set PUREMLIP_LAMMPS to the `lmp` binary and PUREMLIP_LAMMPS_MODEL_DIR to the
directory holding the exported models and the exporter's versions.txt, then run
`julia --project=PureMLIP/test PureMLIP/test/reference/run_lammps.jl`.

The reference code in test/reference/ is original to this repository. MACE is MIT
licensed (Copyright (c) 2022 Ilyes Batatia, Gregor Simm and David Kovacs); LAMMPS is
GPL-2.0-only; the numerical values listed here are outputs of those programs.

On macOS, set HWLOC_COMPONENTS=-darwin to run `lmp`: Open MPI's hwloc topology
discovery otherwise hangs in MPI_Init.

Sign convention: virial = p·V/nktv2p = -dE/dε (eV) with the kinetic part excluded and the
LAMMPS sign (positive pressure is compressive); the stress dE/(V·dε) is therefore -virial/V.
